# <type>(<scope>): <short imperative summary>
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 🎯 Motivation
# What problem does this solve, and why is this change needed?
#
# ✨ Changes
# - Describe the resulting behavior, not a list of edited files.
# - Explain important implementation decisions or tradeoffs.
#
# 🧪 Validation
# - ✅ Passed: <exact command or manual check and observed result>
# - ⏭️ Not run: <relevant check and reason>
# - ❌ Failed: <check, failure, and remaining action, if applicable>
#
# ⚠️ Impact & compatibility
# Describe affected consumers, API/data/config changes, and known limitations.
# Call out required environment variables or dependencies when applicable.
#
# 🚀 Rollout & rollback
# Describe deployment order, migrations, or manual steps when needed.
# Explain how to recover; identify changes a simple revert cannot undo.
#
# 👀 Reviewer context
# Highlight non-obvious decisions, follow-up work, or evidence worth checking.
# Link only real screenshots, logs, or recordings; omit if unnecessary.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Footer block (optional; keep these keys unchanged):
# Refs: <real issue or ticket reference, if applicable>
# BREAKING CHANGE: <incompatible change and required migration, if applicable>
#
# 📝 Writing guide — remove this guidance from the final message
# - Types: feat, fix, docs, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore, revert.
# - Scopes: auth-service, api-gateway, workspace, docs, ci, docker, or another owner.
# - Scope is optional. Aim for a subject of 72 characters or fewer.
# - Keep the subject in conventional form; icons belong in the body sections.
# - Use ! before the colon for breaking changes: feat(api-gateway)!: ...
# - Keep a blank line between the subject, body sections, and footer block.
# - Keep only useful sections; a small change may need just a subject and reason.
# - Remove placeholder text, unused validation outcomes, and divider lines.
# - Include only actual validation and real references; never invent results.
# - Do not include credentials, private user data, or unrelated changes.
# - Lines starting with # are discarded during normal interactive commits.
